export default "## Understanding the Post-Period Transition\n\nA period does not end on a clean line. The endometrium, the uterine lining, sheds over several days. The final traces of shedding appear as increasingly brown, light discharge in the last one to two days of the period. When this brown discharge lingers for a day or two after the main flow has stopped, it is usually just the tail end of the same process.\n\nWhere it gets worth paying attention is when new spotting appears after a clear break from the period. If you have had several spotting-free days and then notice fresh pink or light red blood, that is a distinct event.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only. It is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## Residual Shedding: The Normal End of a Period\n\nThe most common reason for spotting right after a period is that the period has not fully ended. The endometrium sheds in waves, and the last bits may emerge as brown or pinkish discharge for a day or two after heavier flow has stopped. This is not a separate event. It is the period concluding.\n\nThis type of residual spotting:\n- Is brown, not red\n- Is very light, just traces, not staining through clothing\n- Resolves within one to two days\n- Is not accompanied by cramping or other symptoms\n\nIf this describes what you are experiencing, it is normal and does not need investigation.\n\n## Ovulation Spotting\n\nFor some people, the approach to ovulation brings a small amount of spotting. This happens for two reasons. First, the brief estrogen dip that precedes the LH surge just before ovulation can cause the uterine lining to shed a small amount. Second, the physical rupture of the follicle as the egg is released can cause a small amount of bleeding that sometimes reaches the vaginal canal.\n\nOvulation spotting typically appears mid-cycle, around 10 to 16 days after the start of your last period. It is:\n- Pink or light red\n- Brief, lasting one to two days\n- Sometimes accompanied by one-sided pelvic pain\n- Consistent in timing cycle to cycle for people who are prone to it\n\nIf you are tracking your cycle and notice spotting around the midpoint, ovulation is a likely and benign explanation.\n\n## Cervical Sensitivity and Ectropion\n\nThe cervix is more vascular than it appears from outside. Contact from penetrative sex, a pelvic exam, or a cervical cup can cause a small amount of cervical bleeding. This appears as pink or light red spotting within hours to a day of the contact.\n\nCervical ectropion, where the softer glandular cells normally inside the cervical canal extend to the outer surface, makes this more common. Ectropion is a benign variant. Many people have it without knowing, and it is more prevalent during pregnancy, with hormonal contraceptive use, and in younger people.\n\nPost-sex spotting in the follicular phase is often cervical ectropion. It is worth mentioning to a clinician during a routine exam but rarely requires treatment.\n\n## Hormonal Contraceptives\n\nBreakthrough bleeding and mid-cycle spotting are recognized side effects of hormonal contraceptives. This is particularly true with progestin-only pills, implants, hormonal IUDs, and injectable contraceptives. Spotting may appear at various cycle points, including after a withdrawal bleed.\n\nIf you are using hormonal contraception and have consistent post-period spotting, a review of the method or dose may be appropriate. This is especially true if the spotting began when you started or changed a contraceptive.\n\n## Uterine Polyps and Fibroids\n\nBoth polyps, which are soft growths of endometrial tissue, and fibroids, which are muscular growths in the uterine wall, can cause irregular bleeding patterns. This includes spotting that appears after a period has ended. This spotting is often:\n- Recurring consistently rather than occasionally\n- Not clearly tied to a specific cycle phase\n- Sometimes accompanied by heavier periods overall\n\nIf post-period spotting is new, consistent, and not explained by contraceptive effects or identifiable cervical sensitivity, ultrasound is the appropriate next step to assess for structural causes.\n\n## What to Log\n\nFor post-period spotting, record:\n\n- **Date** and approximate cycle day\n- **Relation to period**, how many days after the period fully stopped\n- **Color**, brown for likely residual, pink, or red\n- **Amount**, trace, pantyliner, light\n- **Duration**, how many days it lasted\n- **Any context**, sex in the preceding 24 hours, a recent pelvic exam, new hormonal contraceptive\n- **Whether it recurs** at the same point in subsequent cycles\n\nTwo to three cycles of this data is usually enough to identify whether a pattern exists.\n\n## The Privacy Dimension\n\nSpotting and cycle irregularity data, when stored on an app's servers, is accessible to the company and potentially to law enforcement or data brokers. Period tracking data is not protected by HIPAA. That law applies to healthcare providers and insurers, not consumer apps. Flo Health's FTC enforcement action and subsequent $59.5 million class action settlement demonstrated that major apps have shared sensitive user data without adequate disclosure.\n\nAn on-device tracker stores your cycle data only on your device. The company never receives it, so there is less company-held data to produce in a legal proceeding and no third-party pipeline through which it can flow. For reproductive health tracking, that architectural distinction is meaningful.";
